3.1 Random-Access Ranked Retrieval ( RAR )
Given a scoring function 𝑓, we define a total order over the dataset
Dby sorting the points in descending order of their scores. For
example, in Figure 1, the ordering of the projections on the vec-
tor of𝑓shows their ranking. Let rankD,𝑓:D→Ndenote the
function that assigns to each point in Dits rank in this ordering
(i.e., rankD,𝑓(𝑝)=𝑖if𝑝is the𝑖-th highest-scoring point under
score𝑓). When the dataset Dis clear from the context, we write
rank𝑓for brevity. We denote by 𝑝(𝑖)the𝑖-th point (aka the 𝑖-th
element) under score𝑓, that is,𝑝(𝑖)=rank−1
𝑓(𝑖),∀1≤𝑖≤𝑛.
The main problem is to efficiently find the point at a specific rank 𝑖.
Problem 1 ( 𝜅-Random-Access Ranked Retrieval ( 𝜅-RAR)).
Given a datasetDavailable at preprocessing time, a query consists
of a linear scoring function 𝑓∈R𝑑and an integer 𝑖∈[𝑛], find a
conformal setC(𝑖)⊂D , where|C(𝑖)|=𝜅and𝑝(𝑖)∈C(𝑖).
Based on this definition, when 𝜅=1, the task reduces to identify-
ing the exact 𝑖-th ranked element. As shown in Appendix L, finding
𝑝(𝑖)in high-dimensional spaces is challenging, as its complexity
is tied to that of the half-space range counting problem [ 2,26]. For
1<𝜅≪𝑛, this formulation naturally leads to a relaxed variant
of the problem: instead of returning a single point, the output is a
small set that is guaranteed to contain 𝑝(𝑖). Inspired by the notion
of conformal prediction in machine learning [ 8,85], we refer to this
set as the conformal set for rank𝑖.6
3.2 Stripe Range Retrieval ( SRR)
While addressing Problem 1, we also solve a side problem that
finds all points that fall in a stripe range. Formally, a stripe range
S𝑓,ℓ,𝑢 is defined by a scoring function 𝑓∈R𝑑and two real-valued
boundariesℓ,𝑢∈Rsuch thatℓ≤𝑢. It corresponds to the universe
of all points 𝑥∈R𝑑whose scores based on 𝑓lies within the interval
[ℓ,𝑢], i.e.,
S𝑓,ℓ,𝑢={𝑥∈R𝑑|ℓ≤score𝑓(𝑥)≤𝑢}.
