3.3 Random-Access Similarity Search ( RAS)
Similar to the ranked retrieval problem, we define a random-access
variant of the similarity search problem (also known as nearest
neighbor search) under a given similarity metric.
Formally, let d:D×D→ Rbe a distance function that induces
the similarity metric on the dataset D. For a query point 𝑞, we define
a ranking function nn_rank𝑞:D→N,which orders points in D
by decreasing similarity to 𝑞(equivalently, by increasing distance
d(𝑞,·)). In other words, the most similar point to 𝑞(i.e., the nearest
neighbor) has rank 1.
Despite the classical NN problem that retrieves the 𝑘-nearest
neighbors for a constant 𝑘, we consider a more general random-
access problem, retrieving the point in rank 𝑖for any𝑖∈[𝑛].
Problem 3 ( 𝜅-Random-Access Similarity Search ( 𝜅-RAS)).
Given a datasetDand a similarity metric davailable at prepro-
cesing time, a query consists of a point 𝑞∈R𝑑, and an integer
𝑖∈ [𝑛], find a conformal set C(𝑖) ⊂D , where|C(𝑖)|=𝜅and
𝑝(𝑖)=nn_rank−1𝑞(𝑖)∈C(𝑖).
Similar to Problem 1, when 𝜅=1, we look for the exact 𝑖-th point,
while for 1<𝜅≪𝑛, we have a relaxed version of the problem.6
In this work, we focus on two widely used distance metrics in
ANN search and practical similarity search systems: the ℓ2−norm
(Euclidean distance) and cosine similarity.
In the following, we first show how the RAS problem can be
reduced to RAR . Subsequently, we focus on solving RAR , which
represents the more general problem.
3.4 Reducing RAS to RAR
In this section, we use existing techniques to reduce the RAS prob-
lem to the RAR problem, and show that an efficient solution to the
RAR problem would also solve the RAS problem.
Euclidean distance : Given a query point 𝑞∈R𝑑, theℓ2−norm
similarity of a point 𝑝∈D to𝑞is measured as d(𝑝,𝑞)=∥𝑝−𝑞∥2.
