4 Background
In this section, we summarize the key concepts and definitions used
in this paper. We assume to be given a range space (D,R), where
Dis the dataset, andRis a collection of geometric ranges defined
overD, such as all possible balls, half-spaces, or other ranges.
VC-Dimension . The Vapnik-Chervonenkis (VC) dimension [ 95]
for a range space is the size of the largest subset 𝐶⊆ D such
that every subset of 𝐶can be realized as the intersection of 𝐶with
some range inR. A low VC-dimension implies a limited expressive
power, which in turn allows for efficient sampling-based approxi-
mations [50].
𝜀-Samples . An𝜀-sample of a range space (D,R)is a subsetN⊆D
such that, for every range 𝑅∈R, the proportion of points in 𝑅is
approximately preserved in N. More formally,Nis an𝜀-sample if
for all𝑅∈R,|︁|︁|︁|︁|𝑅∩D|
|D|−|𝑅∩N|
|N||︁|︁|︁|︁≤𝜀.
