Enclosing Balls . For each node 𝑝at layerℓ, we defineBℓ(𝑝)as the
smallest enclosing ball that covers the area Aℓ(𝑝). These balls are
later used during query time to enable efficient pruning of nodes
when searching within a stripe range:
Bℓ(𝑝)=Enclosed-Ball(Aℓ(𝑝)).
Construction Algorithm . To construct the hierarchical graph
G(D) , we employ a bottom-up recursive approach. Starting from
the base layer, we iteratively sample a subset of points to form the
next layer. At each level, we connect nodes in the current layer to
their nearest centroids in the layer above, thereby forming directed
edges. After establishing the connections, we compute and update
the area sets and corresponding enclosing balls for the newly con-
structed layer. The pseudo-code of the preprocessing procedure is
detailed in Algorithm 3 in Appendix K. Please refer to the Appen-
dix J for the analysis of this algorithm.
Querying Algorithm . Given a stripe range S𝑓,ℓ,𝑢, the goal is to
report all points inS𝑓,ℓ,𝑢∩D. The query process begins at the top of
the hierarchy, starting from layer L𝐿. At each layer ℓ, we examine
whether the enclosing ball Bℓ(𝑝)of each point 𝑝∈Lℓintersects the
stripeS𝑓,ℓ,𝑢. If there is no intersection, the corresponding node is
pruned from further exploration. Otherwise, we proceed to explore

