E Our Algorithm Based on 𝑘-th Level of
Arrangement
In this section, we describe the details of KthLevel algorithm. We
begin by introducing key concepts from computational geometry,
and then describe our algorithm in detail.
E.1 Preliminaries
Duality . We use the classic dual space transformation that maps
points to hyperplanes and vice versa. This transformation is well-
defined and preserves intersection relationships [ 29,41]. Formally,
the dual of a point 𝑝∈R𝑑is defined as:
dual(𝑝):={︂
x∈R𝑑|x⊤𝑝=1}︂
.
In other words, the transformation dual(·)maps a point in the
primal space to a hyperplane in the dual space . This hyperplane is
represented by the equation:
ℎ:𝑑∑︂
𝑖=1𝑝[𝑖]·𝑥𝑖=1.
We refer to the original input domain as the primal space , and
the space of dual hyperplanes as the dual space . Figure 11 provides
a visual illustration of this transformation. The left figure shows the
primal space, while the middle figure shows the dual hyperplanes
in the dual space. The following key observation follows from this
duality. A formal proof can be found in [41].
Lemma 7. Let𝑓be a scoring function (i.e., a direction vector), and
let𝑝(1),...,𝑝(𝑛)be the points sorted in descending order according
to their score under 𝑓, so that𝑓⊤𝑝(1)>𝑓⊤𝑝(2)>···. Then, for any
𝑎<𝑏, the intersection point of dual(𝑝(𝑏))with the ray in direction
𝑓lies closer to the origin than that of dual(𝑝(𝑎)). □
For instance, in Figure 11, the point 𝐵has the highest score under
𝑓1, and thus its dual dual(𝐵)intersects the ray 𝑓1in the dual space
earlier (closer to origin) than any other point’s dual.
E.1.1𝑘-th Level of Arrangement. Consider the set of dual hyper-
planesH={dual(𝑝) |𝑝∈ D} .Hdefines a dissection of R𝑑
into connected convex cells, called the arrangement of the hyper-
planes [41].
To simplify the explanations, let 𝑑=2. Sweep a ray 𝑓coun-
terclockwise from the 𝑥-axis to the 𝑦-axis. At each orientation of
𝑓, the ray intersects all dual hyperplanes (dual lines) in H. These
intersection points can be sorted by their distance from the origin.
The𝑘-th level is the locus of points that lie on the 𝑘-th closest dual
line intersected by 𝑓, as the direction of 𝑓varies continuously [ 41].
In general𝑑-dimensional space, consider a ray 𝑓originating at
the origin and sweeping over all directions on the unit sphere S𝑑−1.For each direction 𝑓, the ray intersects the hyperplanes in Hin
up to𝑛distinct points, which can again be ordered by increasing
distance from the origin. The 𝑘-th level is the set of all such 𝑘-th
intersection points across all directions 𝑓∈S𝑑−1.
Figure 11 illustrates the first and second levels of the arrangement
inR2, shown in orange and green, respectively.
E.2 KthLevel Algorithm
In this subsection, we formally present our algorithm, KthLevel .
We begin by describing the preprocessing phase, followed by a dis-
cussion of the query phase , and conclude with a theoretical analysis
of the algorithm’s performance. The overall structure of the algo-
rithm applies uniformly across dimensions 𝑑≥2; however, for the
purpose of analysis, we distinguish between the planar case ( 𝑑=2)
and the general higher-dimensional case ( 𝑑>2).
Preprocessing . During the preprocessing phase, we construct the
arrangement of the dual hyperplanes induced by the point set D,
and the levels of the arrangement for all 1≤𝑘≤𝑛. We assume
access to an oracle Kthat, for a given 𝑖, returns an efficient data
structure representing the 𝑖-th level of the arrangement [ 41]. By
calling this oracle for each 𝑖∈ [𝑛], we obtain the complete set
of structures needed for query processing. Each such structure
allows constant-time access to the hyperplanes intersecting on
each face of the arrangement, as well as the points in the primal
space corresponding to those hyperplanes [41].
Query Phase . In the query phase, we are given a scoring function
𝑓and a rank𝑖, and the goal is to retrieve the point 𝑝(𝑖), which is
the𝑖-th highest-ranked point with respect to 𝑓. By Lemma 7, the
intersection of the 𝑖-th level of arrangement of dual hyper-planes
with𝑓corresponds with the desired point 𝑝(𝑖). Algorithm 1 shows
the pseudo-code of our process for finding this intersection.
Consider the 3rd level of the arrangement highlighted (in orange)
in the Figure 11 (right figure). In 2D, the 𝑘-th level is a non-convex
chain of line segments, each belonging to a dual line. Now, let
the query function be 𝑓2, while𝑖=3. Our objective is to find
𝑝(3)=rank−1
𝑓2(3). The line segment in the 3rd level that intersects
with the vector of 𝑓2isdual(𝐷). Hence, the point 𝑝(3)=𝐷in this
example. Note that applying a binary search on the endpoints of
the line segments of the 3rd level, one can find the intersection of
any function 𝑓with any level 𝑖in a time logarithmic to the number
of line segments.
From the above example, observe that the 𝑘-th level is a chain of
line segments. Similarly, for a general 𝑑≥2, the𝑘-th level is a chain
of(𝑑−1)-faces. A𝑑′-face of an arrangement is a 𝑑′-dimensional
convex cell, formed by the intersection of 𝑑−𝑑′hyperplanes. For
example, 0-faces are vertices (points), 1-faces are edges (segments
or lines), and so on.
Each function 𝑓, in such cases, is an origin-anchored vector that
intersects one of the faces of each level 𝑖,∀𝑖∈[𝑛]. Projecting the
endpoints of these faces on the surface of the unit sphere S𝑑−1forms
an arrangement in the (𝑑−1)-dimensional space. For example,
when𝑑=3, each𝑘-th level is a chain of plane segments, and
their projection onto the unit ball (centered at the origin) is an
arrangement of lines.

