H Dynamic Setting
In this section, we discuss the dynamic setting. We consider two
types of dynamic operations: Insert(p) andDelete(p) . For the
KthLevel algorithm, these operations are supported following the
literature on dynamically maintaining levels of arrangements [ 4,24].
Below, we focus on the EpsHier algorithm; analogous results can
be shown for EpsRange .
Insert(p) . This operation adds a new point 𝑝to the datasetD.
ForEpsHier , we must update both the 𝜀-sampleN𝜀and the hi-
erarchical sampling structure described in Section 6.3. Following
Theorem 3, we want to maintain an unbiased random subset of size
|N𝜀|ofDas an𝜀-sample. Our goal is to maintain such a random
subset without resampling the entire dataset. This can be efficiently
achieved using reservoir sampling [96], which allows us to main-
tain a uniform random sample as new elements arrive. To update
the hierarchical structure used for the SRR problem, we apply this
reservoir sampling procedure bottom-up, from the base layer to
higher layers. At each layer, we decide whether to include the new
point, as each layer is a random sample of the one below it. This
process takes 𝑂(log𝑛)time since the index contains log𝑛layers.Delete(p) . This operation removes 𝑝fromD. Under the backing
sample method [ 47], if𝑝∈N𝜀, we delete it from the sample. If the
sample size drops below a predefined lower bound 𝐿, we rebuildN𝜀
by resampling all the points. The amortized time for this operation
(based on the definition of 𝐿) is𝑂(1)[47]. For the hierarchical
structure, each layer ℓis maintained similarly, with its own bound
𝐿ℓ, and each layer ℓis the sample maintained at level ℓ−1. Hence, a
rebuild at level ℓonly scans the immediately lower level, not the
entire dataset, keeping the amortized cost low. In other words, this
rescanning at layer ℓtakes𝑂(|Lℓ−1|)=𝑂(𝑛
2ℓ−1), but the amortized
is still𝑂(1)for this layer and it takes 𝑂(log𝑛)to resample all the
top layers. As a result, the total amortized time for this operation is
𝑂(log𝑛)for the hierarchical structure.
I Analysis of the EpsRange Algorithm
The analysis is similar to Eps2D , however, the time complexity of
