L Lower Bounds and Optimality of Our
Algorithms
In this section, we analyze the complexity of the RAR problem and
show the near-optimality of our proposed algorithms. Specifically,
the well-studied half-space range counting problem [ 2,26] reduces
to our problem. Hence, the complexity of the state-of-the-art algo-
rithms for half-space range counting provides a lower bound for
theRAR problem.
The half-space range counting problem is defined as follows:
given a point set in R𝑑and a query half-space 𝐻, determine the
number of points lying within 𝐻.
Lemma 11. LetAbe an algorithm that solves the RAR problem
with query time 𝑇(A) and space complexity 𝑆(A). Then, usingA,
one can solve an instance of the half-space range counting problem
using space𝑆(A) and query time Θ(log𝑛·𝑇(A)) .
Proof. To count the number of points within a given half-space
𝐻, we can perform a binary search over 𝑘∈{1,2,...,𝑛}. In each
iteration, we query Ato retrieve the 𝑘-th ranked point 𝑝(𝑘). If
𝑝(𝑘)∈𝐻, then the count of points in 𝐻is at least𝑘; otherwise, it is
less. This process requires Θ(log𝑛)queries toA, resulting in total
query time Θ(log𝑛·𝑇(A)) . □
It is known that the query time for half-space range counting in
R𝑑with𝑚units of space satisfies the following lower bound [26]:
Ω(︃𝑛
𝑚1/𝑑log𝑛)︃
